      "message": "Note: I had to add writable_data_nocheck() as well.\n\nWe have multiple places (in the old code) where clients just manually casted away the constness. When I replaced those with writable_data(), the \"check\" fired -- their object had more than one owner...\n\nBut this was fine for them: they (e.g.) might have stashed away a ref to the data somewhere else, and then wanted to write into it.\n\nI\u0027ll do whatever you guys want -- my 2cents is that I think this was always a flawed idea that we could enforce single-ownership for writing. I think we\u0027re in C++, and we should say that a \u0027const SkData\" is immutable, and that a SkData isn\u0027t, and let the language (and our clients\u0027 discipline) take it from there.\n\nBut, as I said, I will do whatever you want for this CL.",
